          <li class="flex gap-2">
            <span style="color:var(--jfo-accent)">→</span>
            <span data-i18n-html="homeToolMinifier"><a href="/json-minifier" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1);font-weight:600">JSON Minifier</a> — Strip all whitespace and compress JSON to its smallest valid form. Shows exact byte savings and compression percentage.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-2">
            <span style="color:var(--jfo-accent)">→</span>
            <span data-i18n-html="homeToolCsv"><a href="/json-to-csv" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1);font-weight:600">JSON to CSV Converter</a> — Transform any JSON array of objects into a spreadsheet-ready CSV. Keys become column headers; download as a <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">.csv</code> file.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-2">
            <span style="color:var(--jfo-accent)">→</span>
            <span data-i18n-html="homeToolDiff"><a href="/json-diff" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1);font-weight:600">JSON Diff Checker</a> — Compare two JSON documents side by side. Colour-coded additions, deletions, and changes with full dot-notation key paths.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider" style="color:var(--jfo-accent);font-family:ui-monospace,monospace"><span data-i18n="homeFaqTitle">Frequently Asked Questions</span></h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq1Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">What is JSON format?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq1A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) format is a lightweight, text-based data interchange format. It represents data as key-value pairs enclosed in curly braces <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">{}</code> for objects and square brackets <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">[]</code> for arrays. JSON is language-independent, human-readable, and the most widely used format for transmitting data between a server and a web application.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq2Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">What is a JSON file format?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq2A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">A JSON file is a plain text file saved with the <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">.json</code> extension that stores data in JavaScript Object Notation format. JSON files are used to transmit structured data in web APIs, store application configuration, and exchange data between systems. They can be opened with any text editor and parsed by virtually every modern programming language.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq3Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">What is the full form of JSON?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq3A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">JSON stands for <strong style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">JavaScript Object Notation</strong>. Despite the name, JSON is completely language-independent — it is supported natively in Python, Java, PHP, Ruby, Go, Rust, and virtually every other programming language, making it the universal standard for data exchange on the web.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq4Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">What does JSON format look like?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq4A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">JSON uses curly braces for objects and square brackets for arrays. Keys must be double-quoted strings. Values can be strings, numbers, booleans, null, objects, or arrays. Example: <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">{"{"}\"name\": \"Alice\", \"age\": 30, \"roles\": [\"admin\", \"editor\"]{"}"}</code>. A <strong>JSON formatter online</strong> makes this structure easy to read by adding indentation and line breaks.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq5Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">What is a JSON formatter?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq5A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">A <strong>JSON formatter</strong> is a tool that takes raw, compact, or minified JSON and reformats it with consistent indentation and line breaks — also called pretty-printing or beautifying. It makes JSON data human-readable without changing the underlying data. A <strong>JSON formatter online</strong> does this instantly in your browser with no installation required.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq6Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">How to format JSON?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq6A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">To format JSON online: 1) Paste your JSON text into the input panel. 2) Choose your indentation style (2 spaces, 4 spaces, or tab). 3) Click the Format button. The tool parses the JSON and outputs a neatly indented, syntax-highlighted version. You can then copy the formatted output or download it as a <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">.json</code> file.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq7Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">How to properly format JSON data?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq7A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">Properly formatted JSON data follows these rules: all keys must be double-quoted strings; string values must use double quotes; no trailing commas after the last item in an object or array; no comments; no <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">undefined</code> values. Use a <strong>free JSON formatter and validator</strong> to check and fix formatting automatically.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq8Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">How to beautify JSON format?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq8A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">To beautify JSON, paste your minified or raw JSON into a <strong>pretty JSON formatter</strong> and click Format. The formatter runs <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">JSON.parse()</code> to validate the data, then <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">JSON.stringify()</code> with an indentation argument to produce clean, indented output with each key on its own line — also called JSON pretty-printing.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq9Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">How to use a JSON formatter?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq9A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">Using this <strong>free online JSON formatter</strong> takes three seconds: 1) Open the site. 2) Paste your JSON into the left input panel, or click Load File. 3) Select your indentation (2sp, 4sp, or Tab). 4) Click ⚡ Format. The right panel shows syntax-highlighted output. 5) Click Copy or Download. Your input is automatically remembered when you switch tabs.</p>
