          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq10Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">How does a JSON formatter work?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq10A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">A <strong>JSON formatter</strong> works in two steps. First, it passes your input through <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">JSON.parse()</code>, which validates the syntax and builds an in-memory data structure — reporting the exact line and column of any error. Second, it serializes the data back with <code style="font-family:ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:0.85em;color:var(--jfo-accent)">JSON.stringify()</code> and an indentation parameter, producing neatly formatted output.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq11Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">What are the benefits of using a JSON formatter?</h3>
            <p data-i18n-html="faq11A" class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color:var(--jfo-text-2)">The main benefits: (1) <strong>Readability</strong> — transforms dense single-line JSON into structured, scannable text. (2) <strong>Error detection</strong> — validates syntax and pinpoints errors with line and column numbers. (3) <strong>Debugging</strong> — makes it easy to inspect API responses and config files. (4) <strong>Productivity</strong> — saves time compared to reading minified JSON manually. (5) <strong>Privacy</strong> — a client-side formatter never sends your data to any server.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 data-i18n="faq12Q" class="text-sm font-semibold" style="color:var(--jfo-text-1)">What is the best JSON formatter online?</h3>
